## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying a simple bitwise identity (Gray code) across a non-native 128-bit representation, including cross-boundary shifts. It enforces branch-free, constant-time thinking and careful hi/lo handling.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using 64-bit halves
- Gray code relation: G = X ^ (X >> 1)
- Cross-boundary shifts between hi and lo

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 toGray128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hiGray = x.hi ^ (x.hi >> 1);
  uint64_t loGray = x.lo ^ ((x.lo >> 1) | (x.hi << 63));
  return (uint128){.hi = hiGray, .lo = loGray};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness with randomized tests without branches?
- What’s the throughput difference when implemented on 128-bit wide arithmetic vs emulated two 64-bit halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests non-branching bit-twiddling and packing/unpacking across a mask, a common performance pattern in high-throughput code.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and order-preserving packing
- Non-branching compression via cascade masks
- Fixed-sequence bit-manipulation tricks

## Code Example
```javascript
function bitpack64(v, mask){
  let x = v & mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & 0x5555555555555555n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & 0x3333333333333333n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8n)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16n)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32n)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFn;
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support dynamic masks without recompilation?
- Compare performance with a loop-based approach on large data sets.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness understanding and safe 128-bit lane manipulation with pure bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Byte reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct arithmetic
- Endianness swapping

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft compact, branchless bit-twiddling across a 128-bit type.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit split into hi/lo; XOR fold reduces width.
- Branchless reduction using successive shifts and XORs.
- Final parity is the low bit after folding.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation in C-like syntax for readability
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t t = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  t ^= t >> 32;
  t ^= t >> 16;
  t ^= t >> 8;
  t ^= t >> 4;
  t ^= t >> 2;
  t ^= t >> 1;
  return (unsigned int)(t & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you compute the full popcount for 128-bit without branches?
- How does this approach fare on different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level arithmetic with 128-bit values, ensuring correct carry propagation and branchless coding. It mirrors real-world bit-twiddling tasks in crypto, compression, or hash pipelines where performance and correctness matter.

## Key Concepts

- Carry propagation across 64-bit boundaries
- 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo
- Branchless implementation using comparisons as carry indicators

## Code Example

```c
uint128 add128(uint128 a, uint128 b, unsigned *carryOut) {
  uint64_t lo = a.lo + b.lo;
  unsigned c0 = lo < a.lo;
  uint64_t hi = a.hi + b.hi + c0;
  unsigned c1 = hi < a.hi;
  *carryOut = c1;
  return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

